## Why This Is Asked
New angle: per-block setup cost while selecting up to k blocks, non-overlapping; tests ability to derive a compact DP with prefix sums and a best-so-far trick; includes reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost
- Prefix sums to compute block values quickly
- Best-so-far technique to achieve O(nk) time
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKBlocksWithCost(A, k, cost) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) P[i] = P[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  bestVal[0] = 0; // dp[0][0] - P[0]

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const candidate = P[i] - cost + bestVal[t-1];
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], candidate);
    }
    // update bestVal for next i
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const val = dp[i][t-1] - P[i];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) bestVal[t] = val;
    }
  }
  return { dp, P };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem evaluates proficiency in tree DP with disjoint-path packing and exact-path-count constraints, along with path reconstruction. It combines knapsack-on-tree techniques with path endpoint handling while guaranteeing vertex-disjointness.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP with multiple states per node (unused vs. endpoint vs. interior)
- Knapsack-style merging across children via DP table convolution
- Path reconstruction through stored decision pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfs(u, parent) {
    const dp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), dp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
    dp0[u][0] = dp1[u][1] = 0;
    for (const v of children[u]) {
        dfs(v, u);
        const ndp0 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp1 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF), ndp2 = Array(k + 1).fill(-INF);
        for (let i = 0; i <= k; i++) for (let j = 0; j + i <= k; j++) {
            ndp0[i + j] = Math.max(ndp0[i + j], dp0[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp1[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp1[i + j] = Math.max(ndp1[i + j], dp1[u][i] + max(dp0[v][j], dp2[v][j]));
            ndp2[i + j] = Math.max(ndp2[i + j], dp2[u][i] + dp0[v][j]);
        }
        dp0 = ndp0; dp1 = ndp1; dp2 = ndp2;
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced tree DP with a connectivity constraint and a knapsack-like merge across children. Requires careful reconstruction of the chosen nodes.

## Key Concepts

- Tree DP: dp[u][s] for connected subgraphs containing u within its subtree
- Knapsack-like merging of children to combine subtree sizes
- Path reconstruction: storing decisions to recover node set
- Complexity: O(nk) time and O(nk) space; handle negative values with offset

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxConnectedSubtreeSum(n, k, edges, val) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { g[u].push(v); g[v].push(u); }
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  const order = [];
  function dfs(u, p) {
    parent[u] = p;
    for (const v of g[u]) if (v !== p) dfs(v, u);
    order.push(u);
  }
  dfs(0, -1);
  // dp[u] will be an array of length min(subtreeSize[u], k) + 1
  // Initialization, merging and reconstruction would follow a standard tree knapsack pattern
  // Full implementation is lengthy; outline focuses on the recurrence above
  return { best: null, nodes: [] };
}
```

